schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: Grassroots Economics' Sarafu network scaled from vouchers, not a token launch
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Grassroots Economics (the Kenyan nonprofit behind the Sarafu network) did
    not launch with a token economy — Sarafu began as community members
    issuing simple vouchers, and only migrated onto the Celo blockchain after
    the model had proven itself on the ground. The network now supports tens
    of thousands of small businesses across Kenya, Cameroon, and South
    Africa; in a 2025 survey of active users, 95% said the network is
    important or very important to their household economy.
  evidence: >-
    Cited in the article's References to "Grassroots Economics, Sarafu
    Network" (https://www.grassrootseconomics.org/sarafu-network), "Why
    Celo" (https://www.grassrootseconomics.org/celo-ge), and "2025 Sarafu
    Network Study Results" (https://grassecon.substack.com/p/2025-sarafu-network-study-results).
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/minimum-viable-node.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used in the article as the concrete real-world example of "earning your
    way onto a blockchain" — the technology as an upgrade to a working
    community practice, never a substitute for it.
  uncertainty: >-
    Figures (survey percentage, business counts, country coverage) as
    reported in Grassroots Economics' own materials; not independently
    re-verified for this ingestion.
  high_risk: true
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/minimum-viable-node.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
